<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/13th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mongol conquest of the Jin dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">from <a href="/years/1211">1211</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mongol conquest of the Jin dynasty was the series of campaigns from 1211 in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> and his successors destroyed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchen</a> Jin state in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. The Jin capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhongdu">Zhongdu</a> fell in 1215, and the dynasty ended in 1234. The conquest brought northern China under Mongol rule, laying the base for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan Dynasty</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mongol-conquest-of-the-jin-dynasty.jpg" alt="Mongol conquest of the Jin dynasty" width="320" height="196" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sayf al-Vâhidî. Hérât. Afghanistan · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bataille_entre_mongols_%26_chinois_(1211).jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchen</a> rulers of the Jin dynasty took tribute from some of the steppe tribes and encouraged rivalries among them. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> were united under Khabul in the 12th century, the Jin urged the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a> against them, and the Tatars later handed Khabul's successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambaghai">Ambaghai</a> to the Jin court, where Emperor Xizong had him executed. In 1210 an embassy announced the accession of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wanyan-yongji">Wanyan Yongji</a> and demanded that the Mongols submit as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> reportedly spat on the ground and rode away, and in early 1211 he summoned a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurultai">kurultai</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kherlen-river">Kherlen River</a> and declared war.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In 1211 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> broke through the Jin wall at Wushabao and defeated the Jin field army at Yehuling, while the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jebe">Jebe</a> took the Eastern capital, present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaoyang">Liaoyang</a>. Campaigns in 1212 and 1213 followed. In 1214, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhongdu">Zhongdu</a> besieged, the Jin agreed to pay tribute and gave Genghis Khan Princess Qiguo in marriage, but Emperor Xuanzong then moved the capital south to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Kaifeng</a>. The Mongols returned, and Zhongdu fell on 1 June 1215. They suppressed resistance in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanxi">Shanxi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebei">Hebei</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a> from 1217 to 1223, while the general Muqali died besieging Feng County in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaanxi">Shaanxi</a>.</p><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ogedei-khan">Ögedei Khan</a> the Jin, led by Emperor Aizong, won victories at Dachangyuan in 1229, Weizhou in 1230 and Daohuigu in 1231. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> then converged on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Kaifeng</a> from three sides, Tolui's force passing through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> territory, and destroyed Wanyan Heda's army in a snowstorm at Sanfengshan. Aizong fled from Kaifeng to Guide, where Pucha Guannu's fire-lance troops won a night attack, and then to Caizhou. The Southern Song allied with the Mongols, and Caizhou was besieged from both sides. When its walls were breached Aizong killed himself, and his successor, Emperor Mo, was killed within a day, on 9 February 1234.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-chinese">Han Chinese</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khitans">Khitans</a> had gone over to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> during the war. Defectors such as Shi Tianze, Zhang Rou and Yan Shi commanded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tumen">tumens</a> of 10,000 men under Ögedei and helped build the administration of the new state. The Mongols spared cities that surrendered, and ordered that physicians, craftsmen and clerics be spared and brought to them. James Waterson cautions against blaming the fall in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s population on slaughter alone, since many people may have moved south to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> or died of disease and famine. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fire-arrow">fire arrows</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchens</a> used at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Kaifeng</a> in 1232 were a weapon the Mongols adopted for later conquests.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mongol conquest of the Jin dynasty” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
